  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThis afternoon a frontal boundary was pushing from west to east \nacross the U.P. with GOES visible satellite imagery showing \nwidespread cloud cover accompanying the front and NEXRAD radar \nindicating a narrow band of showers along the leading edge. These \nshowers were very light and little in the way of measurable rainfall \nwas occurring as they passed through the central and eastern U.P. \nSurface observations showed northerly wind along the north shore of \nLake Superior along a rather ill defined cold front. Temperatures \nhad warmed into the 50s and 60s with much of the western U.P. under \nsunny skies while clouds associated with the front were moving \nthrough the eastern U.P. Winds were light from the southwest except \nthey had turned northwest over the Keweenaw. In the upper levels \nmodel height analysis and satellite water vapor imagery showed \nridging starting to amplify over the Northern Plains ahead of a \ncutoff low moving towards California.\n\nBy this evening winds will shift northeasterly over the U.P. as a \nweak cold front drops south across the area. Residual low level \nmoisture that was advected into the region today along with moisture \nfrom the Great Lakes will combine with weak, mainly upslope flow and \nradiational cooling to develop widespread fog over the U.P. tonight. \nThere is the potential that the fog could be dense, especially over \nthe eastern half of the U.P. Commuters should leave extra time to \nreach their destination tomorrow morning as the fog is likely to \nlinger into the mid-morning hours. Thereafter the fog will lift but \nlow clouds will linger over the northern half of the U.P. into the \nearly afternoon with continued flow off of the Great Lakes and a \nstrong subsidence inversion, limiting mixing. Outside of the clouds \ntemperatures will warm into the upper 60s (western U.P.) while area \nnear Lake Superior will only reach the upper 50s (still several \ndegrees above average). \n\nThe amplifying ridge will shift into the Great Lakes region for the \nend of this week as the main energy from the western low moves \nnortheast to Saskatchewan and phases with the main jet stream. The \nridge then flattens our with zonal flow redeveloping along the \nUS/Canadian border on Sunday as the low pushes east to the Hudson \nBay. Expect warm weather through the weekend, especially on Saturday \nas temperatures reach into the low to mid 70s over the western U.P. \nCooler, 50s to low 60s, can be expected Sunday as a cold front \nsweeps across the U.P. There continues to be uncertainty with how \nmuch moisture will be available with the front and probabilities for \n>0.10\" of rainfall remain low, ~25%. There is the potential for \nshowers to linger into Monday and Tuesday with some guidance showing \nthe frontal boundary stalling across the region. Confidence is high \nthat we will see much cooler temperatures for Monday and Tuesday \nwith NBM probabilities for highs of <50F around 75-95%. The \ndeterministic guidance has highs in the mid to upper 40s Monday and \nTuesday, which, believe it or not, is right around average for late \nOctober.",
